/* 2026-08-08: 🚌 Getting Around + 🚗 Food Delivery — entries now read SIDE BY SIDE (owner rule 2026-08-08). Both sections stacked each entry as a name row above a body row, so a two-word card like "🚕 Uber" / "uber.com" burned two full rows and a section of three ride apps read as six stacked strips instead of three facts. Each section is now a 2-column grid: the entry name (.extras-sub) is the left column, its body (.transit-box — the operator link, plus the description rows a transit mode carries) is the right, and the two halves join into one white card per row (radius 4px 0 0 4px / 0 4px 4px 0, matching 1.5px transparent borders so both halves sit at the same height and the first text lines align). .extras-title spans both columns and keeps its own card shape and its −28px pull-out. Column 1 is fit-content(38%): it sizes to the LONGEST name in the section so every link in that section lines up in one vertical run, and the 38% ceiling stops a long transit name ("🚢 Chao Phraya Express Boat") from squeezing its description to one word per line on a phone — measured at 393px, the worst case in the fleet, the description keeps ~4-5 words a line. Both halves of the FIRST entry row take the 16px title gap so the card's top edge is level (the shared .extras-title + * rule reaches only the left one). Verified on Athens, Bangkok, Lucerne, Geneva and San Juan Island at 1410px and 393px; zero horizontal overflow. Locked by brain_check check_getting_around_side_by_side (6 invariants + the retired stacked shape banned) and, per guide, by validate_itinerary's side-by-side entry rows check. Fleet scan: 410 sections across 215 guides already carry the alternating .extras-sub/.transit-box pairs the grid needs — 0 guide HTML edits. guide-style.css -> v135. CACHE to v349. */
/* 2026-08-08: ⭐ Best Of — every shipped guide now has the section (owner rule 2026-08-08). The section is injected from CITY_BEST_OF_MAP, which build_best_of_map.py derives ONLY from href="../Guides/{City}/…" links inside Best-*.html, so 71 of the 215 shipped guides had no Best Of section at all: 24 were described by a card that simply never carried the 🌐 link, 6 more were named after a landmark that is a numbered stop in the guide but sits outside the city (Neuschwanstein/Marktoberdorf, Stirling Castle/Glasgow, Joshua Tree/Palm Desert, Lençóis Maranhenses/São Luís, San Juan Islands whale watching/Orcas Island + San Juan Island), and the remaining 41 had no qualifying card anywhere, so entries were authored for them across 19 Best-Of pages with Commons photos. Map regenerated by auto_update_best_of_map.py: 187 -> 234 cities, 651 city×page pairs, 0 shipped guides without a section. toolbar.js -> v223. CACHE to v350. */
/* 2026-08-08: Motion banner sits FLUSH against the stop it departs from (owner rule 2026-08-08). The banner was already pulled up to the card above (margin-top -10px, 2026-08-06), but the card ends with .stop-photos and that strip carries a 14px bottom margin, so 14 - 10 left a 4px sliver of the day-block's #fdf8f0 showing between the card's sealed white bottom edge and the banner's #f5f0e6 - measured at exactly 4px on all 95 stop-to-banner joins across Athens, Bangkok, Lucerne, Tokyo, Berlin and Busan. At that width it does not read as spacing, it reads as a stray background stripe running under the card, which is what the owner flagged. Zero is now set at the SOURCE rather than by widening the negative pull to -14px: on a stop that carries a banner, the photo strip drops its 14px bottom margin, the stop card drops its own 8px, and the banner drops its 8px top margin - so the join stays at 0 even if one of those three spacings is later re-tuned, where a -14px pull would silently re-open a gap. Selected with :has(+ .next / + .next-tram / + .next-metro) so only banner-carrying stops are touched; a stop with no banner keeps its 8px. The 8px BELOW the banner (.next + .stop-block) is unchanged, so the banner still groups upward to the stop it departs from. Re-measured after the change: 0px above and 8px below on all 95 joins at both 1410px and 393px. check_card_in_section_layout's -10px invariant is replaced by three invariants covering the new flush join. guide-style.css -> v136. CACHE to v351. */
/* 2026-08-08: Motion banners go stone, lounge chip goes terracotta (owner rules 2026-08-08, options D and 3 of that session's mockups). All five motion banners — .hotel-first, .arrive-first, .next, .next-tram, .next-metro — move off warm beige (#f5f0e6/#7a5c1e) to a neutral stone (#f2f1ec/#5a564a): transit is plumbing between stops, and on the warm palette a walking time carried the same weight as the places it connected, so the eye stopped at every banner instead of skipping to the next stop. All five share one fill because the :has(+ .next-tram) glue collapses the radius and margin between a banner and its tram sub-line and depends on there being no visible seam. Dark twin moves with it, #232221/#9d988c (5.6:1), off the #222120/#c8a060 gold every other guide foreground uses — the light side left the warm family, so the dark side does too. The lounge chip goes the other way: #fbf0ea fill, #b85c2a rail, #7a3b1e ink, the site accent, so the one actionable thing at the top of Day 1 is deliberately NOT in the same family as the plumbing beneath it (it was gold for one commit, 6066faad, before the owner changed it). Dark twin #2a211c/#d4874a, the palette's established dark lift of the accent. Also fixes a bug the gold exposed but the original teal always had: the chip is an <a>, and the global `a, a:visited { color: var(--c-link) }` is specificity 0-1-1, so it outranked the 0-1-0 class rule and repainted the IATA code and airport name link-blue after the first click, fill and rail staying put. `a.lounge-arrival-chip:visited` (0-2-1) locks it, in both dark blocks too. brain_check gained _anchor_classes_from_js so this class of bug is caught by machine: check_universal_visited_color_match only saw classes the CSS writes as `a.classname`, which never includes a chip built by toolbar.js, so the missing lock was invisible to it. guide-style.css -> v137. CACHE to v352. */
/* 2026-08-08: Guide foot controls are ONE row (owner rule 2026-08-08). The "N days" pill sat bottom-right with the scroll-top arrow stacked directly above it, and because the mobile .container is nearly full-width both floated over the page rather than over a gutter — so a reader who scrolled to the very end stopped with the round gold FAB parked on the last card. On Tallinn that is the "Gothenburg" Nearby-Guides pill: a gold circle sitting on a gold pill, which is what the owner reported as a doubled pill in the corner. The pill is now centred (left:50% + translateX(-50%)), which frees the right corner, and the arrow drops beside it — both at bottom:23px, putting their centres on the same optical line as #tve-nav-back (34px tall at bottom:24px), so "← Back" · "N days" · ↑ read as one row. Shrinking the band from 98px to 59px takes .container's mobile bottom padding from 110px to 72px (59 + 13px gap). Row geometry verified at 320/360/393/430px on Tallinn and on Zurich (10 days, the fleet's widest label): all three share one centre line, zero horizontal overlap, smallest gap 18px, and nothing but container padding sits under the controls at true page bottom. Locked by brain_check check_mobile_container_clears_float_band (MOBILE_FLOAT_BAND_PX 59). guide-style.css -> v138. CACHE to v353. */
/* 2026-08-08: Stop hours — the slab's own padding IS the row gap (owner rule 2026-08-08, "make sure the space is right between the items in the stop"). Every plain row in a stop card sits 6px from its neighbour, but the band is the one row with a tinted box around its text — 6px of padding inside it — so a 6px margin ON TOP of that padding put the band's TEXT 12px from the next row's text while every other pair sat at 6px. Measured on Austin's Ellsworth Kelly card: 35px band-to-next pitch against 29px for every other pair in the same card, which is the step the owner's screenshot shows. The band's vertical margins go to 0 and the row immediately after it drops its own margin-top (otherwise the band's padding-bottom and the neighbour's margin-top stack straight back up to 12px), so every text pair in the card is on the same 6px rhythm and the tint extends 6px past its own text the way a full-bleed tinted row should. NOTE this exact change was made and reverted earlier the same day as "glued to the line above and below with no edge between them" — that band was INSET, with no rail on the card edge and no right border; the slab now carries a 2.5px rail, a right hairline and a full-bleed span, so it reads as its own object without needing a margin to prove it. Verified on Austin and Athens: mid-card bands measure 0px box gap above and below with 6px interior padding, i.e. symmetric 6px text pitch on both sides; 393px shows zero horizontal overflow. toolbar.js -> v224. CACHE to v354. */
